      <h2>Your Complete Thai Keto Shopping List</h2>
      <p>Walking into an Asian grocery store on keto can be overwhelming. The aisles are filled with unfamiliar ingredients, and it's not always obvious which ones fit your macros. This comprehensive list covers every Thai ingredient you need for keto cooking — and flags the ones to avoid.</p>

      <h2>Proteins (All Keto-Friendly)</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Chicken thighs:</strong> The preferred cut for Thai curries and stir-fries — more flavorful and forgiving than breast.</li>
        <li><strong>Pork shoulder/butt:</strong> For slow-cooked dishes and grilled moo ping skewers.</li>
        <li><strong>Ground pork:</strong> Essential for pad kra pao (Thai basil stir-fry) and larb.</li>
        <li><strong>Shrimp:</strong> The star of tom yum soup and pad Thai. Buy shell-on for better flavor.</li>
        <li><strong>Beef sirloin or flank:</strong> For Panang curry and Thai beef salad (yum nua).</li>
        <li><strong>Fish fillets:</strong> For steamed fish with lime and chili (pla neung manao).</li>
        <li><strong>Eggs:</strong> For Thai omelets (khai jiao) and fried eggs on top of stir-fries.</li>
        <li><strong>Tofu (firm):</strong> Moderate carbs — use sparingly. About 2g net carbs per 100g.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Low-Carb Vegetables</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Thai eggplant:</strong> Small, round, and slightly bitter. About 4g net carbs per cup.</li>
        <li><strong>Chinese broccoli (gai lan):</strong> Similar to broccolini. Excellent in stir-fries.</li>
        <li><strong>Bok choy:</strong> Mild, crunchy, and very low-carb.</li>
        <li><strong>Green beans:</strong> Classic in Thai curries. About 4g net carbs per cup.</li>
        <li><strong>Bamboo shoots:</strong> Canned is fine — rinse well. Very low-carb.</li>
        <li><strong>Bean sprouts:</strong> For pad Thai garnish. About 3g net carbs per cup.</li>
        <li><strong>Zucchini:</strong> Great curry vegetable and noodle substitute.</li>
        <li><strong>Cabbage:</strong> For stir-fries and as a rice substitute.</li>
        <li><strong>Mushrooms:</strong> Straw mushrooms for tom yum, shiitake for stir-fries.</li>
        <li><strong>Bell peppers:</strong> Red and green — use in moderation (3-4g net per half pepper).</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Herbs & Aromatics (Zero to Negligible Carbs)</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Thai basil (horapha):</strong> Anise-like flavor. Essential for green curry and pad kra pao.</li>
        <li><strong>Cilantro:</strong> Both leaves and roots. Roots are used in curry pastes.</li>
        <li><strong>Mint:</strong> For larb and Thai salads.</li>
        <li><strong>Lemongrass:</strong> Bruise and add whole to soups and curries.</li>
        <li><strong>Galangal:</strong> Similar to ginger but more citrusy. Don't substitute ginger.</li>
        <li><strong>Kaffir lime leaves:</strong> The most important Thai aromatic. Buy frozen if fresh isn't available.</li>
        <li><strong>Garlic:</strong> Use freely — the carbs are negligible in the amounts used.</li>
        <li><strong>Shallots:</strong> Use in moderation — about 3g net carbs per shallot.</li>
        <li><strong>Thai bird chilies:</strong> Tiny but fiery. Use to taste.</li>
        <li><strong>Green and red Thai chilies:</strong> For curry pastes and garnish.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Sauces & Condiments</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Fish sauce (nam pla):</strong> 0g carbs. The most important Thai seasoning. Red Boat is the gold standard.</li>
        <li><strong>Coconut aminos:</strong> 1g net carb per tsp. Soy sauce alternative for keto.</li>
        <li><strong>Shrimp paste (kapi):</strong> 0g carbs. Fermented shrimp paste for curry pastes and dips.</li>
        <li><strong>Tamarind paste:</strong> Use sparingly — about 3g net carbs per tablespoon. Essential for pad Thai sauce.</li>
        <li><strong>Rice vinegar:</strong> 0g carbs. For dipping sauces and dressings.</li>
        <li><strong>Lime juice:</strong> Fresh only. About 1g net carb per lime.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Pantry Staples</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Full-fat coconut milk:</strong> 3-4g net carbs per 1/2 cup. Aroy-D and Chaokoh are excellent brands.</li>
        <li><strong>Coconut cream:</strong> Even higher fat, lower carbs than coconut milk.</li>
        <li><strong>Thai curry pastes:</strong> Maesri and Mae Ploy are the best keto options.</li>
        <li><strong>Konjac noodles:</strong> 0g net carbs. The best noodle substitute for pad Thai.</li>
        <li><strong>Cauliflower rice:</strong> 2g net carbs per cup. The go-to rice substitute.</li>
        <li><strong>Erythritol or monk fruit:</strong> For replacing palm sugar in recipes.</li>
        <li><strong>Toasted sesame oil:</strong> A few drops for finishing stir-fries.</li>
        <li><strong>Crushed peanuts:</strong> For garnish. About 2g net carbs per tablespoon.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Ingredients to Avoid or Limit</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Palm sugar:</strong> Pure sugar. Always substitute.</li>
        <li><strong>Sweet soy sauce (kecap manis):</strong> Loaded with sugar.</li>
        <li><strong>Oyster sauce:</strong> Contains sugar and cornstarch. Use sugar-free versions or omit.</li>
        <li><strong>Hoisin sauce:</strong> Very high in sugar. Avoid entirely.</li>
        <li><strong>Sweet chili sauce:</strong> Almost entirely sugar. Make your own.</li>
        <li><strong>Rice noodles:</strong> Pure carbs. Use konjac or zucchini noodles.</li>
        <li><strong>Jasmine rice:</strong> The obvious one. Cauliflower rice is your friend.</li>
        <li><strong>Spring roll wrappers:</strong> Made from rice flour and tapioca.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

